{"about":"7043957a83082eac14fe82fd2fee22d8e8267cf00f35e5ac504f3b9944aae437","body":"The conjecture asserts a pragmatic inconsistency where none exists. Popper's system allows for a 'rational choice' among unfalsified theories based on their content and testability, not on an inductive guarantee of success. Acting on the best-corroborated theory is consistent with fallibilism; it does not require the assumption that past survival predicts future truth, only that it is the most critically examined option available, making the charge of smuggling induction back in a category error regarding the function of corroboration.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Alleged pragmatic inconsistency"}